## 3. An unbounded-domain estimate for the exact prepared orbit

The weak-field vector approximation alone does not permit applying an unbounded electric operator to its error. The following graph estimate avoids that step.

For either prepared input, the parent's explicit residual gives

\[
 \|H_g\phi_{\nu,g}\|\le C_0/\tau_*.
 \tag{10}
\]

Indeed \((H_g I_g-I_gH_{\rm h})\psi=O(g^2)\) in Hilbert norm, \(H_{\rm h}\psi\) is a fixed finite Hermite vector, and the normalization tends to one exponentially. Since \(H_g\) is self-adjoint on \(D(Q)=H^2(\mathcal Q)\), its exact unitary evolution preserves this domain and its \(H_g\) graph norm. Thus (10) holds for \(\phi_{\nu,g}(s)\) at every s.

For a smooth vector on the compact flat quotient, integration by parts gives

\[
 \begin{split}
 \|H_g\phi\|^2={}&K^2\|Q\phi\|^2+\|V_g\phi\|^2
       +2K\int V_g|\nabla\phi|^2
       -K\int(\Delta V_g)|\phi|^2.
 \end{split}
 \tag{11}
\]

The potential is nonnegative and

\[
 K\|\Delta V_g\|_\infty
 \le\frac1{2\tau_*^2}\sum_p\|c_p\|^2=:C_\Delta/\tau_*^2,
 \tag{12}
\]

independently of g. Smooth functions are a core for Q; at each fixed g the smooth potential and its derivatives are bounded. Approximation in the Q graph norm extends (11) and the following inequality to \(D(Q)\):

\[
 K^2\|Q\phi\|^2+\|V_g\phi\|^2
 \le\|H_g\phi\|^2+K\|\Delta V_g\|_\infty\|\phi\|^2.
 \tag{13}
\]

Consequently the **actual**, rather than harmonic-reference, prebirth orbit satisfies

\[
 \sup_s\|Q\phi_{\nu,g}(s)\|\le C_Qg^{-2}.
 \tag{14}
\]

There is no appeal to a uniform graph-norm estimate for the vector-norm approximation error. The all-s bound (14) does not extend the parent's boundary-contrast approximation beyond its fixed time interval.

Now consider a local birth map. It is a finite sum of matter partial isometries times integer electric translations \(E\mapsto E+\zeta\), with bounded, finite-support translation vectors \(\zeta\). This follows directly from \(B_j=Pj_jF_{a_0}P\) and the finite number of legal star hops; the normalized rotor path weights have magnitude at most one. Each relevant coefficient of D obeys a bound

\[
 0\le D(q',E+\zeta)\le C_\zeta\left(1+\sum_eE_e^2\right).
 \tag{15}
\]

For example, use \(|x(x\pm1)|\le2x^2+1\) and
\(|E+\zeta|^2\le2|E|^2+2|\zeta|^2\), then sum the finitely many edges. These are pointwise electric-basis estimates; matter occupancy restrictions can only remove summands. Summing the finitely many translation terms gives

\[
 B_jD(Q)\subset D(D_+),\qquad
 \|D_+B_j\phi\|\le C_j(\|Q\phi\|+\|\phi\|).
 \tag{16}
\]

This is valid on the full physical postbirth space, including changed charges or winding. No initial-sector identity \(D_+=Q\) is assumed. One may establish (16) first on finite electric words and then pass to the Q graph closure. Since D is closed and the birth maps are bounded, this also proves the domain inclusion.

Combining (14), (16), fixed-graph boundedness of \(H_4^C,\Lambda\), and (1), yields

\[
 \sup_{0\le s\le T,\ \nu\in\{0,\alpha\}}
 \|A_{+,g}\eta_{\nu,g}(s)\|
 \le\frac{C_A}{\tau_*g^2}.
 \tag{17}
\]

Here fixed \(\kappa\tau_*\) is absorbed into \(C_A\). More explicitly the electric contribution is O(\(\tau_*^{-1}\)), the bounded fourth-order contribution is O(\(\tau_*^{-1}g^{-2}\)), and the loss contribution is O(\(\kappa\)). The last two are not dropped. The norm of \(\eta\) is exactly \(\sqrt5\) by the provisional initial effect; a generic fixed bound would also suffice. No estimate of the electric graph norm of the evolved postbirth state is required.

## 4. Full no-event semigroup and the finite-bin remainder

For each fixed g, \(h_{+,g}\) is self-adjoint on \(D(D_+)\) by bounded perturbation. Adding the bounded nonnegative loss makes \(A_{+,g}\) a contraction-semigroup generator on the same domain. This follows, for example, by the bounded dissipative perturbation of the skew-adjoint generator; its Trotter construction alternates unitary propagation and the contractions \(e^{-\kappa u\Lambda_+/2}\). Hence, for \(\eta\in D(A_{+,g})\),

\[
 \|(e^{uA_{+,g}}-I)\eta\|
 \le\int_0^u\|e^{vA_{+,g}}A_{+,g}\eta\|dv
 \le u\|A_{+,g}\eta\|.
 \tag{18}
\]

The domain inclusion in (16) justifies applying this identity to the actual first-birth vectors. For a bounded effect \(M_l=B_l^*B_l\), contraction and (18) give

\[
 \begin{split}
 &\left|\|B_le^{uA_{+,g}}\eta\|^2-\|B_l\eta\|^2\right|\\
 &\qquad\le2\|B_l\|^2\|\eta\|\,u\|A_{+,g}\eta\|.
 \end{split}
 \tag{19}
\]

Thus, uniformly in \(s\in I\), both preparations, and \(u\ge0\),

\[
 |f_{\nu,g}(s,u)-f_{\nu,g}(s,0)|
 \le C_F\kappa^2e^{-\lambda s}\frac{u}{\tau_*g^2}.
 \tag{20}
\]

The bound is useful at short lags; no small-lag expansion of an unbounded operator in operator norm has been made. Integration gives the central estimate

\[
 \left|P_\nu(g;I,b)-b\int_I f_{\nu,g}(s,0)ds\right|
 \le\frac{C_F}{2}\frac{\kappa^2 L_Ib^2}{\tau_*g^2}.
 \tag{21}
\]

This estimate retains the full postbirth generator and all no-event losses. It is also valid when some postbirth components are dark to further births.

## 5. Conditional finite-bin vacuum and packet contrasts

The provisional readout input supplies, uniformly on the fixed interval,

\[
 \begin{split}
 f_{0,g}(s,0)
   &=\kappa^2e^{-\lambda s}
       [23+2e^{-g^2v_p/2}+O_T(g^4)],\\
 f_{\alpha,g}(s,0)-f_{0,g}(s,0)
   &=\kappa^2e^{-\lambda s}
       [-2g^2e^{-g^2v_p/2}|\chi_p(s)|^2+O_T(g^4)].
 \end{split}
 \tag{22}
\]

In particular the O(\(g^4\)) expectation accuracy in (22), stronger than a bare O(\(g^2\)) vector error, is an imported premise from that source. It is not derived by this finite-bin check. If its proof or the exact effect changes, (22) and the consequent contrast must be revisited; the graph and semigroup estimates remain separately usable.

Define

\[
 L_I=\int_Ie^{-\lambda s}ds>0,\qquad
 \Xi_I=\int_Ie^{-\lambda s}|\chi_p(s)|^2ds\ge0.
 \tag{23}
\]

Equations (21)-(22) give

\[
 \begin{split}
 P_0={}&\kappa^2bL_I[23+2e^{-g^2v_p/2}]
       +O(\kappa^2bL_Ig^4)
       +O(\kappa^2L_Ib^2/(\tau_*g^2)),\\
 P_\alpha-P_0={}&-2\kappa^2bg^2e^{-g^2v_p/2}\Xi_I
       +O(\kappa^2bL_Ig^4)
       +O(\kappa^2L_Ib^2/(\tau_*g^2)).
 \end{split}
 \tag{24}
\]

The two remainder constants may differ. Under (A),

\[
 P_0\sim P_\alpha\sim25\kappa^2L_Ib,
 \qquad
 \frac{P_\alpha-P_0}{\kappa^2bg^2}\longrightarrow-2\Xi_I,
 \tag{25}
\]

and, if \(\Xi_I>0\),

\[
 \frac{P_0-P_\alpha}{P_0}
     =\frac{2\Xi_I}{25L_I}g^2[1+o(1)].
 \tag{26}
\]

The normalized remainder in the contrast is bounded by constants times
\(L_I[g^2+b/(\tau_*g^4)]\). Thus the sufficient window is explicit. One example is \(b(g)=\tau_*g^{4+\zeta}\) for any fixed \(\zeta>0\). For a sufficiently small fixed prefactor multiplying \(\tau_*g^4\), a sign can also be obtained if the actual remainder constants and \(\Xi_I\) are known, but the universal coefficient limit (25) here uses the little-o condition (A). No values for the cube constants are certified by the toy controls below.

The survival factor in (23) is essential. It can be very small for late I or large V; it is not replaced by one. The selected first-event probability is exactly \(5\kappa L_I\) for either preparation. Therefore this is a two-event deficit relative to a common coincidence background, not a first-event-rate signal or an efficiency claim.

If \(\chi_p\) vanishes identically, \(\Xi_I=0\) and no leading signal follows. Conversely a finite sum of exponentials is analytic; if it is not identically zero, its squared magnitude has positive weighted integral on every nondegenerate interval. Destructive cancellation within frequency-degenerate subspaces can make a packet dark at the selected plaquette. A zero-length I or \(\kappa=0\) also removes the signal; the main result assumes both are excluded.

At fixed g, strong continuity gives the usual limit as \(b\downarrow0\), regardless of (A). It does not give a g-uniform contrast for a fixed b. This proof leaves possible sharper windows or the actual cube's fixed-lag behavior unresolved. It is not a no-go theorem for other readouts, preparations, or windows.

## 6. Counts under an explicitly supplied repetition assumption

Assume N independent preparations of each type under identical parameters, with independent resets and mark observations between all trials. This is an additional experimental/probabilistic arrangement, not a property of repeated events in one evolving finite record system. Let \(C_\alpha,C_0\) count (4) in the two arms, and \(D_N=C_\alpha-C_0\). Then exactly

\[
 \begin{split}
 \mathbb E D_N&=N(P_\alpha-P_0),\\
 \operatorname{Var}(D_N)
     &=N[P_\alpha(1-P_\alpha)+P_0(1-P_0)].
 \end{split}
 \tag{27}
\]

For (A) and \(\Xi_I>0\),

\[
 \mathbb E D_N\sim-2N\kappa^2bg^2\Xi_I,
 \quad \operatorname{Var}(D_N)\sim50N\kappa^2L_Ib,
 \quad
 \frac{|\mathbb ED_N|}{\sqrt{\operatorname{Var}D_N}}
 \sim\frac{2\kappa\Xi_I}{\sqrt{50L_I}}g^2\sqrt{Nb}.
 \tag{28}
\]

For a desired order-one signal-to-standard-deviation ratio z, the leading repetition count per arm is

\[
 N\asymp\frac{25z^2L_I}{2\kappa^2 b g^4\Xi_I^2}.
 \tag{29}
\]

This is an elementary count scaling, not a false-alarm calibration. Chebyshev already shows concentration around the nonzero mean relative to its magnitude when
\(N\kappa^2 b g^4\Xi_I^2/L_I\to\infty\); no Gaussian approximation is needed for that statement. If \(b=\tau_*g^{4+\zeta}\), the fixed-parameter scaling in (29) is \(g^{-(8+\zeta)}\). The costly repetition scaling and survival suppression remain explicit. Background knowledge, detector errors, dark counts, packet preparation fidelity, and independent physical resetting are not supplied by this mathematical estimate.

## 7. Required microscopic labeled-process comparison and order of limits

The target estimates above are on the common rotor process. They do not follow for microscopic mark times from unconditioned density convergence alone. In particular the microscopic bare jump annihilates the fully occupied initial A pattern at time zero, while its effective initial hazard is nonzero. Differentiating a density-convergence statement at zero or shrinking b before taking the microscopic limit would not be justified.

At each **fixed** \(g>0\), \(b>0\), graph and time horizon, the local-compensation parent supplies the spin scaling

\[
 \epsilon^2=\frac{\delta}{KS(S+1)}
            =\frac1{2g^4S(S+1)}.
 \tag{30}
\]

The prescribed spin-cutoff approximations to the prepared initial states converge in trace norm at fixed g. The parent's strong spin-to-rotor result uses bounded perturbations of the same diagonal KD. Its microscopic comparison and its sentence about finite event/count registers remain conditional imported results, not re-proved here.

The needed bridge is convergence of the **same original labeled first/next-event probability**

\[
 P_{\nu,\mathrm{micro},S}(E_{jl}(I,b))
       \longrightarrow P_\nu(g;I,b)
 \quad\text{for each fixed }(g,b),\quad\nu=0,\alpha.
 \tag{31}
\]

This can be established by a sufficiently strong comparison of the first-two-mark Janossy measures, or by a registered version of the generalized target theorem that retains the original labels and no-event histories. Merely comparing total event counts, the reduced field density, the full unlabelled system density, or only the first formation does not supply (31).

There is a concrete finite-register reduction of the time condition; one need not silently assume a finite register stores a continuous first-event time. Partition a horizon exceeding \(T+b\) into bins of width at most \(\eta\), include the endpoints of I, and store: no event yet, the first event's label/time-bin, accepted, or failed. The second event's current time-bin gives a lower and upper possible lag. Using bins certainly inside or possibly intersecting the conditions in (4) gives finite-register events
\(E^-_\eta\subset E_{jl}(I,b)\subset E^+_\eta\).
The update rules are deterministic classical updates attached to the original jump labels; they do not alter the unobserved system instrument. Finite piecewise time schedules suffice.

For the rotor target, contraction and the fixed-graph jump bounds give
\(f_{\nu,g}(s,u)\le\kappa^2\|B_l\|^2\|B_j\phi_{\nu,g}(s)\|^2
\le125\kappa^2\)
for the selected resolved marks. Thus the ambiguous strips around the first-time endpoints, lag zero, and lag b have mass tending to zero with \(\eta\); for fixed I and b one may bound it by a constant times
\(\kappa^2[\eta(|I|+b)+\eta^2]\).
If the conditional microscopic theorem applies to these finitely many labeled register updates and time pieces, its fixed-grid probability convergence followed by \(\eta\downarrow0\) proves (31) by squeezing. No bound on a microscopic pointwise jump density is required for this squeeze. Verifying this applicability to the original monitored microscopic instrument is the precise remaining bridge; this PRE does not confer it by assuming the reduced-density result is stronger than stated.

The justified conditional order is therefore:

1. Fix graph, \(a,c,\kappa,T,I\), g, and a positive b. Establish the registered microscopic-to-target comparison as \(S\to\infty\) along (30), with the permitted bare P initialization and its physical spin approximants. For the register construction, take this limit at a fixed time grid, then refine the grid.
2. Having obtained (31), take \(g\to0\) with b satisfying (A), keeping the earlier fixed parameters and packet definition fixed.

For a joint sequence \(S=S(g)\) to preserve the nonzero leading contrast, a sufficient quantitative condition is

\[
 \max_{\nu=0,\alpha}
 |P_{\nu,\mathrm{micro},S(g)}(E)-P_\nu(g;I,b(g))|
       =o(\kappa^2 L_I b(g)g^2).
 \tag{32}
\]

At the fixed-parameter level the factor \(\kappa^2L_I\) is a nonzero constant with the proper units. Convergence (31), if established, permits a diagonal choice along a sequence \(g_n\downarrow0\); it does not provide an explicit economical \(S(g)\) schedule. Knowing only \(S\gg g^{-2}\), or an O(\(\epsilon\)) error with unspecified g- and register-dependent constants, is insufficient to verify (32).

Condition (32), with the independent repetition assumption, preserves the leading probability, count mean, variance and signal-to-noise formulas. If instead one wants total-variation approximation of the **entire** two-arm N-trial data distribution, a sufficient further requirement is \(N\max_\nu|P_{\nu,\mathrm{micro},S}-P_\nu|\to0\), by the Bernoulli product coupling bound. That stronger requirement is distinct from comparison of the displayed probability coefficients and moment scalings.

There is no simultaneous continuum/volume limit, no supplied resource rate for these increasingly narrow bins, no source-to-detector theorem, and no empirical agreement statement.

