* Eight finite Fourier-polynomial cases check the integration-by-parts identity (11), its positive-potential graph bound, and four integer electric-translation estimates each. Multiplication by cosine pads the Fourier support, so there is no wraparound or omitted endpoint contribution to these finite-polynomial identities. The largest identity residual is about \(1.54\times10^{-14}\). These are controls of a one-angle version of the explicit identity, not infinite-domain or cube enclosures; the domain proof is analytic in §3.
